==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Cat Machine Mk 2''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Cat Machine Mk 3''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
Cat Machine's main assets are its gargantuan attack power and health. Against most Red and [[:Category:Unstarred Alien Enemies|unstarred Aliens]] enemies, its resistance will make for a near-indestructible wall. Its damage can be put to good use as long as you don't abuse knockback since it might miss its attack. Furthermore, Cat Machine's low range may only allow it to serve as a barrier for [[:Category:Wave Attack Enemies|wave enemies]] to strike at, which is not beneficial to your Cats unless you plan to support it with additional wave-immune  units.

In Normal and Evolved Form, it is in truth nothing but a mediocre tanker that is slower than average. Relying on its damage is hard as well because of its long animation and backswing, which will make it stop to attack the ground behind all of the cats that need to be protected.

==='''True Form'''===
Cat Machine Mk 3 is invaluable for its ability to not only be immune to [[Wave Attack]]s, but to protect units behind as well with its resistance to both Alien and Red enemies. Combined with its resistances, Cat Machine Mk 3 can become a near-indestructible wall against [[Berserkory]], [[Elizabeth the LVIth]], [[Two Can]], [[Youcan]]. However, be wary of its short range: unless constantly protected, Cat Machine may be hit by their initial attack anyways. One must also account for certain Starred Aliens, as their warp ability can easily nullify Cat Machine's protection.

The first two forms had a bad combination of sluggish movement speed and a long attack animation, but in True Form, Cat Machine's movement speed is perfect for an Uber with a long attack animation. Mk 1 and Mk 2 have a bad habit of lagging behind your meatshields and don't catch up to a knocked-back enemy before your other attackers do. Mk 3 is more diligent so it can reliably arrive at the scene in time when it is needed. Once it fires a shot at its victim to knock it back, Mk 3 can catch up to it before your other attackers do. But even so, it still sometimes lags behind because of its long attack.

==='''Talents'''===
Being a tank that depends on its high stats, the HP Buff is a straight improvement but is only worth getting if you do not have [[Catseye]]s or have already leveled Cat Machine Mk 3 up to 50. Move Speed Up is also a good one to upgrade, as it allows Cat Machine Mk 3 to reach the frontlines even faster than other [[:Category:Tanker-class Cats|tankers]], making Cat Machine more like [[Lumina (Legend Rare Cat)|Luminalia]] in a way. Knockback immunity directly counters [[Nimoy Bore]] and [[Red EnerG]], and stages combining Red or Alien traits with [[:Category:Enemies with Knockback ability|enemies with knockback abilities]] such as [[Mr. Mole]]. Its hits will no longer be interrupted by constant streams of [[Trolly Blogger]]s, and it'll stay in front to protect far more consistently.

Unfortunately, Attack Buff and Freeze are a huge waste of NP due to their very high cost and how redundant they are, as Cat Machine often misses its attacks, making them rarely even worth having.

==='''Ultra Talents'''===
These are all useful in their own ways, but they also may not be enough to justify the cost. As a stat-based unit, Cat Machine Mk 3 definitely benefits at level 60, but they may not be worth the exorbitant cost of Ultra Talents.

First is a faster attack rate. Cat Machine Mk 3 never had issues with a long time between attacks, but accelerating these attacks can still help. However, at this point, the sluggish foreswing is the real issue hindering its offensive capabilities. This could also be a double-edged sword, as it is likely to immediately wind up another attack after just attacking when it could move further ahead to launch an attacks. Depending on the situation, this could help or hurt its performance.

Next up is Curse Immunity. While some stages mix Red/Alien enemies with [[:Category:Relic Enemies|Relics]] or the [[Relic Doge Base (Enemy Base)|Relic Doge Base]], such stages are relatively rare. Additionally, they would typically have it tanking unresisted Relic enemies anyhow, which may be an issue even at level 60.

Finally, while Barrier Breaker is always nice to have when dealing with durable [[Barrier]]s like [[UltraBaaBaa]]'s and [[Youcan]]'s, those are the only two Barriers it can't already destroy through raw damage, and there are far too many other options to make this worth being an Ultra Talent. It still works, especially when the Wave Shield niche gives it use against Youcan anyhow, but this Ultra Talent is ultimately replaceable.

